# Impersonate - the keys that may sign a release tag (OpenSSH "allowed signers" format,
# ssh-keygen(1) section ALLOWED SIGNERS). The CI job "release" verifies every release tag against
# the copy of this file on main:
#
#   git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=.github/release-signers verify-tag vX.Y.Z
#   -> Good "git" signature for impersonate-release with RSA key SHA256:Dh488r7YcvACwpsZov0/TASb3v3FRjz4//bwoQCyPwU
#
# The fingerprint is published in SECURITY.md ("Release signing key"): compare it from there and with
# the maintainer's GitHub signing keys (https://api.github.com/users/takenek/ssh_signing_keys). This
# file alone, taken from the same clone as the tag, proves nothing.
#
# One line per key: the principal impersonate-release, the namespace git, the public key without a
# comment. Rotation: add the new key's line and extend the old line's options to
# namespaces="git",valid-before="YYYYMMDD", then publish the new fingerprint in SECURITY.md.
impersonate-release namespaces="git" ssh-rsa 
